---
description: Alias-based imports; no relative ./ or ../ paths in source
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx,js,mjs}
alwaysApply: false
---
# Import Paths

Never use `./` or `../` in imports or re-exports.

**Cross-package** — use the published name: `@ndb/<package>` (e.g. `@ndb/core`, `@ndb/database/env`, `@ndb/database/readonly`).

**Within-package** — use `@<short-name>/<path-from-src>`:

`@api/`, `@web/`, `@core/`, `@database/`, `@platform/`, `@middleware/`, `@logger/`, `@bootstrap/`, `@auth/`, `@mcp/`, `@copilot/`, `@statements/`, `@encryption/`, `@notifications/`

**API test helpers** — `@tests/api/<path>` as defined in root `tsconfig.json`. Core fakes: `@ndb/core/tests` (or `@core/tests` inside `@ndb/core`).

```typescript
// BAD
import { createApp } from "./index";
import { Account } from "../../packages/core/src/domains/account";

// GOOD
import { createApp } from "@api/index";
import { Account } from "@ndb/core";
import { fetchAccounts } from "@web/utils/api/routes/accounts";
```

New packages: add `"@<short-name>/*": ["src/packages/<name>/src/*"]` or `"src/apps/<name>/src/*"` to **root** `tsconfig.json` `paths`, plus a bundler alias for `src/apps/web` when the web app needs it. Cross-package imports use workspace `dependencies` and `package.json` `exports`, not tsconfig paths alone.

## No Inline Import Type Queries

Do not use TypeScript import type queries (`import("module").Type` or `typeof import("module")`) in type positions. Import types at the top of the file instead.

Indexed access on imported types (e.g. `AccountListItem["label"]`) is fine.

```typescript
// BAD
type Context = {
  account: import("@ndb/platform").AccountApi;
};

// GOOD
import type { AccountApi } from "@ndb/platform";

type Context = {
  account: AccountApi;
};
```

For Bun partial mocks, use a top-level type namespace import:

```typescript
// BAD
const actual = await importOriginal<typeof import("@ndb/database")>();

// GOOD
import type * as Database from "@ndb/database";

const actual = await importOriginal<Database>();
```
